Thanksgiving is a harder holiday for blood sugar than Halloween. Halloween's trouble is mostly in one candy bowl. On Thanksgiving, the carbs are all over the table: stuffing, potatoes, rolls, sugary cranberry sauce, and then pie.

I still want the meal, not a sad substitute for it. So I went through the plate one dish at a time and looked for where most of the sugar and starch comes from, then changed that part and left the rest alone.

## The menu

**The turkey.** An [herb-mayo roast turkey](/recipes/thanksgiving-herb-mayo-roast-turkey) is the easy part for blood sugar, since turkey has almost no carbs. A coat of herb mayonnaise, homemade or store-bought, helps the skin brown and holds the herbs in place on the whole bird.

**The stuffing.** [Rye and sage stuffing](/recipes/thanksgiving-rye-sage-stuffing) is the most carb-heavy dish here, at about 18g per serving. It uses dense rye, which I wrote about in [Breads I Like Using](/blog/breads-i-like-using), with plenty of sage, and you can add mushrooms for bigger servings.

**The casserole.** The [healthier green bean casserole](/recipes/thanksgiving-green-bean-casserole) swaps the canned soup for a from-scratch mushroom cream sauce, so it still tastes like the classic. Crispy fried onions still go on top, just like the original.

**The gravy.** [Make-ahead gravy](/recipes/thanksgiving-make-ahead-gravy) is a classic brown roux gravy with only about 4g of carbs per 1/4 cup, and you can make it days before the holiday.

**The cranberry sauce.** [No-sugar-added cranberry orange sauce](/recipes/thanksgiving-cranberry-orange-sauce) takes 20 minutes and has about 6g of carbs per serving, nearly all from the fruit.

**The pie.** [Low-sugar pumpkin pie](/recipes/thanksgiving-pumpkin-pie) has a press-in sunflower seed crust and about 10g of carbs per slice. If you'd rather skip the crust, it comes down to about 6g.

## What it adds up to

A serving each of the turkey, stuffing, casserole, gravy, and cranberry sauce comes to about 43g of carbs, or about 35g net of fiber. A slice of pie brings it to about 53g. Your own target may be different, and these are estimates, so it's worth checking your glucose to see how a meal like this works for you.

## Protein first

Earlier this year I wrote about [what I got wrong in my first year](/blog/one-year-in-what-i-got-wrong), and eating protein before carbs was one of the biggest lessons. It works well at a holiday table. Start with a couple of [deviled eggs](/recipes/halloween-deviled-eyeball-eggs) (leave off the eyeball decorations), then have your turkey and green beans before the stuffing.

If you want more on the table, the [low-carb apple crisp](/recipes/low-carb-apple-crisp) makes a good second dessert, and [cottage cheese cloud rolls](/recipes/cottage-cheese-cloud-rolls) can fill the bread basket.

## A make-ahead timeline

- **Up to a week ahead:** Make the cranberry sauce.
- **Sunday:** Move a frozen turkey to the fridge to thaw.
- **Monday or Tuesday:** Make the gravy, leaving out the drippings until Thursday.
- **Tuesday:** Toast the rye cubes for the stuffing.
- **Wednesday:** Salt the turkey in the morning, then bake the pie, cook the stuffing vegetables, assemble the casserole, and make the herb mayo.
- **Thursday:** Coat and roast the turkey. While it rests, mix the stuffing with its eggs and broth, then bake it alongside the casserole at 375°F. Finish the gravy with the pan drippings right before serving.

Happy Thanksgiving. I hope you get to enjoy the whole table and feel good afterward.
